# Routing: Orchestrator

Purpose:
  Define agent selection and execution routing principles.
  Detailed capability and permission definitions live in @agent/.

Principles:
  Agent = WHO executes.
  Skill = HOW work is done.
  Task = WHAT must be done.
  Policy = WHETHER allowed.
  Agent is execution participant, not source of truth. SDD is source of truth.

Agent:
  id: string
  role: string (exactly one primary role)
  capabilities: [CAP-*]
  skills: [SKILL-*]
  autonomy: 0..4
  trust: TRUST-LOW | TRUST-MEDIUM | TRUST-HIGH

CapabilityMatching:
  score = required_capabilities_matched / total_required_capabilities
  100% match required for mandatory capabilities
  extra capabilities allowed but not required

AgentSelection:
  Factors: capability_match, skill_match, availability, quality, risk_fit, context_fit
  Rule: highest scoring eligible agent is selected
  Rule: only AVAILABLE agents are considered

Availability:
  AVAILABLE, BUSY, OFFLINE, DISABLED

Routing:
  Low-risk tasks MAY use cheaper models.
  High-risk tasks MUST use high-quality models.
  Model selection is abstracted from agent identity.

Policy:
  Agent capability does not imply permission.
  Permission is enforced by policy engine.
  Full authorization chain: Task → Agent Capability → Skill Permission → Task Policy → Agent Policy → Environment Policy → ALLOW/DENY/REQUIRE_APPROVAL

Handoff:
  Contract: task_id, current_state, completed_work, pending_work, artifacts, failures, decisions, context
  Rule: handoff transfers context package, not full context.
  Rule: receiving agent requests additional context if needed.

Isolation:
  Rule: agents MUST be isolated from each other's partial work.
  Rule: agents do NOT negotiate directly — all communication goes through orchestrator.

Failure:
  Agent failure does not imply task failure.
  Task failure requires remediation, not just retry.
  Escalation chain is deterministic. Human escalation REQUIRED after exhaustion.

Rules:
  [RT1] Every agent MUST have exactly one primary role.
  [RT2] Agent responsibility is explicit, not implicit.
  [RT3] Agent scope and permission are separate concepts.
  [RT4] Every task has exactly one owner.
  [RT5] Delegation MUST follow formal contract.
  [RT6] Handoff transfers context package, not full context.
  [RT7] Orchestrator is NOT necessarily an LLM agent — workflow is deterministic.
  [RT8] Agents do NOT negotiate directly — all communication goes through orchestrator.
  [RT9] Parallel agents MUST have synchronization points.
  [RT10] Agent isolation prevents partial work interference.
  [RT11] Reviewer is independent from coder.
  [RT12] Critical tasks require two-agent verification.
  [RT13] Security veto cannot be overridden by majority vote.
  [RT14] Agents have autonomy but NOT authority.
  [RT15] Authority comes from SDD governance, not agent self-declaration.
  [RT16] Partial progress does not automatically mean workflow FAILED.
  [RT17] Agent memory MUST be controlled, versioned, audited.
  [RT18] Agent quotas prevent runaway agents.
  [RT19] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to any output.
  [RT20] Agent MUST NOT use emojis or decorative symbols in headers or titles.

